# English versions of the blog posts in yazilar.py (/en/articles/<slug>/).
# Keys are the Turkish slugs; sources and plant lists come from yazilar.py. Plant links are written {id}.

Y_EN = {
'uyku-icin-kullanilan-bitkiler-monograf-ve-kanit': {
'slug': 'plants-used-for-sleep-monographs-and-evidence',
'baslik': 'Plants used for sleep: why monograph level and trial results do not line up',
'aciklama': 'Valerian holds the stronger monograph but its sleep trials are mixed. Lavender has the sounder trials — but for anxiety, not sleep. The evidence behind the '
            'sleep shelf, the plants that disrupt sleep, and the one interaction they all share — with sources.',
'giris': 'In the plants used for sleep, the level of regulatory acceptance and the results of clinical trials line up the opposite way to what one might expect. The plant '
         'with the stronger monograph has mixed sleep trials; the plant with the sounder trials has evidence for anxiety rather than sleep. This review covers that '
         'mismatch, the evidence for each plant on the sleep shelf, the plants that disrupt sleep, and the interaction the group shares; it contains no dosage '
         'information.',
'bolumler': [
 ('Scope and method',
  ['The plants in this review were selected on two criteria: either an indication related to sleep or nervous tension is covered by a European Medicines Agency (EMA) '
   'herbal monograph, or the catalogue holds a recorded traditional use for that purpose [1]. In addition, plants not used for sleep but recorded as causing insomnia '
   'are treated in a separate section. Parts used, safety information and restrictions are given on each plant’s own record page. Dosage and preparation have '
   'deliberately been left out.']),
 ('Valerian: the stronger monograph, mixed results',
  ['{kediotu}, valerian (Valeriana officinalis), stands highest in this group in regulatory terms. The EMA recognises valerian root for mild nervous tension and sleep '
   'disorders under well-established use [1]. This is the upper tier of the monograph categories and requires clinical data.',
   'The trials, however, are mixed: some find a small improvement in time to fall asleep, others find no difference from placebo [1]. The plant’s regulatory standing '
   'is therefore clearer than the picture drawn by the individual studies.',
   'Its restrictions are mild but definite: it can cause morning drowsiness and headache, it should not be combined with alcohol or sedatives, rare cases of liver '
   'injury have been reported, and it should not be taken before driving.']),
 ('Lavender: the sounder trials, but not for sleep',
  ['{lavanta}, lavender (Lavandula angustifolia), occupies a particular place in this review and is easily misread. Standardised oral lavender oil capsules have been '
   'found superior to placebo in a number of controlled trials — but those trials concern anxiety disorder [1].',
   'The EMA, for its part, recognises lavender for mild stress and sleep under traditional use only [1]. The statement “lavender has clinical evidence” is therefore '
   'accurate, but its subject is not sleep. Where anxiety is what disturbs sleep an indirect connection can be drawn; that is not the same as an effect demonstrated '
   'on sleep itself.',
   'The capsule form can cause belching and nausea and may increase the effect of sedative medicines. The Turkish Medicines Agency (TİTCK) monograph advises against '
   'use during pregnancy and breastfeeding, and caution alongside blood thinners and antiplatelet medicines.']),
 ('Lemon balm, chamomile and linden',
  ['For {melisa}, lemon balm (Melissa officinalis), the EMA recognises mild stress, sleep disorders and mild digestive complaints under traditional use; small clinical '
   'trials have reported improvement in anxiety and sleep [1]. There is a restriction for people taking thyroid medication.',
   '{papatya}, chamomile (Matricaria chamomilla), is widely used for insomnia in folk practice, yet the indications its EMA monograph covers are digestive complaints, '
   'inflammation of the mouth and throat, and skin irritation; sleep is not among them. Small trials on anxiety have been positive [1]. It can cause allergy in people '
   'allergic to the daisy family.',
   'For {ihlamur}, linden (Tilia spp.), the EMA recognises symptoms of the common cold and mild mental stress under traditional use; clinical studies are few [1]. It '
   'has no known side effects, the only firm bar being allergy to the flower; the TİTCK monograph reports an interaction with lithium.']),
 ('Plants that disrupt sleep',
  ['In a complaint of insomnia, what is being taken for other purposes matters as much as what is being taken for sleep. Several plants recorded as causing insomnia '
   'are sold as herbal supplements on the same shelves as the sleep ones.',
   'For {ginseng} (Panax ginseng), insomnia is among the recorded adverse effects; prolonged use at high doses may produce hypertension, insomnia, irritability and '
   'muscle stiffness. {altinkok}, roseroot (Rhodiola rosea), can cause insomnia and restlessness.',
   'Caffeine-bearing plants form a separate cluster: {cay}, tea (Camellia sinensis), {guarana} (Paullinia cupana) and {guayusa} (Ilex guayusa). Guaraná seeds carry a '
   'higher proportion of caffeine than coffee beans, and in energy drinks it may be combined with other stimulants. All three carry restrictions for people with '
   'cardiac rhythm problems, anxiety or insomnia.',
   'The first place to look in a complaint of insomnia may therefore not be a sleep plant but the list of what is already being taken.']),
 ('Ashwagandha: sold on the sleep shelf, evidenced elsewhere',
  ['{ashwagandha} (Withania somnifera) has in recent years become one of the world’s best-selling supplements for stress and sleep. A few small, short controlled '
   'trials have reported reductions in stress and anxiety scores; NCCIH notes that these trials were small and short and that long-term safety is unknown [2].',
   'The safety side is clearer than that uncertainty and runs counter to the sales volume: cases of liver injury have been reported, it may raise thyroid hormones, '
   'and it must not be used in pregnancy because of the risk of miscarriage. Some European countries have restricted its supplements over safety concerns.']),
 ('The one interaction the group shares',
  ['The sedative plants in this review come from different families and contain different compounds, yet they converge on a single line in the interaction tables: for '
   'valerian, lemon balm and lavender alike, a restriction on use alongside alcohol and sedative medicines is recorded.',
   'The practical consequence of this cannot be read off any one plant’s safety profile. Taking several herbal products for sleep together, or adding them to a '
   'prescribed sleep medicine, compounds effects that run in the same direction. The warning on valerian’s record against taking it before driving has the same '
   'ground.']),
 ('Opium poppy and a practice that kills children',
  ['{hashas}, the opium poppy (Papaver somniferum), owes its species name to sleep, and the latex of its capsule contains morphine and codeine. A significant part of '
   'modern analgesics is still derived from the poppy; Türkiye is one of the few countries licensed to produce this raw material [2].',
   'Using the plant as a home remedy for sleep is lethal, particularly in children. The capsule and its latex are addictive and can arrest breathing. Its place in '
   'this review is not as an option that may be reached for, but because it has been used for this purpose throughout history and because that use kills.']),
 ('The difference between monograph types',
  ['The phrases “well-established use” and “traditional use” above are not synonyms. A herbal product reaches the market in the European Union by different regulatory '
   'routes, two of which diverge at the level of the monograph [1].',
   'On the well-established use route (Directive 2001/83/EC, Article 10a), scientific literature must establish that the active substances have been in '
   'well-established medicinal use within the European Union for at least ten years, with recognised efficacy and an acceptable level of safety [1].',
   'On traditional use registration (Article 16a(1)), the plant must have been in use for at least thirty years, of which at least fifteen within the European Union. '
   'No clinical trials are required; sufficient safety data and plausible efficacy are enough [1].',
   'This distinction is also the source of the mismatch this review is about. Valerian sits in the upper category while its individual sleep trials are mixed; '
   'lavender has the sounder trials but holds only a traditional-use registration on the sleep side. A monograph category and the strength of evidence for a single '
   'indication are not the same thing.']),
 ('When to see a doctor',
  ['Insomnia is less often a condition in itself than a symptom of something else. Herbal use should not delay the investigation of that something else.',
   'The following should not be deferred by a herbal product: insomnia lasting more than three months and present on most nights of the week, sleep deprivation severe '
   'enough to impair daytime functioning, pauses in breathing during sleep or loud snoring and choking reported by a bed partner, waking regularly with a morning '
   'headache, a sleep disturbance accompanied by low mood and loss of interest, and restless legs.',
   'For people taking prescribed sleep medicines or sedatives, some of the plants in this review carry compounding interactions; these products should not be combined '
   'without the knowledge of a physician. The interaction information is given on each plant’s own record page.'])]},
'adet-sikayetleri-ve-menopoz-icin-kullanilan-bitkiler': {
'slug': 'menstrual-complaints-and-menopause-plants-evidence-and-risk',
'baslik': 'Plants used for menstrual complaints and menopause: evidence, restrictions, and a lethal tradition',
'aciklama': 'Chaste tree’s well-established use in premenstrual syndrome, traditional records for period pain, black cohosh in menopause — and the plants recorded as '
            '“bringing on a period” that have ended in death. With sources; no dosage information.',
'giris': 'Some of the plants used for menstrual complaints also appear in folk records as means of “bringing on a period” or of ending a pregnancy. Those plants are '
         'included in this review — not as an option, but because deaths have been reported. For someone seeking to end a pregnancy these plants are not a route; in '
         'Türkiye that is a procedure carried out by a physician within the legal period. This review covers recorded uses for period pain, premenstrual syndrome and '
         'menopausal symptoms, and the level of evidence behind them; it contains no dosage information.',
'bolumler': [
 ('Scope and method',
  ['The plants in this review were selected on two criteria: either the relevant indication is covered by a European Medicines Agency (EMA) herbal monograph, or the '
   'catalogue holds a recorded traditional use in this area [1]. In addition, plants that appear in folk records here but must not be used because of their toxicity '
   'are treated in a separate section.',
   'Three distinct conditions appear together here and should not be conflated: period pain (dysmenorrhoea), premenstrual syndrome (PMS) and menopausal symptoms. '
   'These are different presentations and their recorded uses differ accordingly. Parts used, safety information and restrictions are given on each plant’s own record '
   'page; dosage and preparation have deliberately been left out.']),
 ('Chaste tree: well-established use in premenstrual syndrome',
  ['{hayit}, chaste tree (Vitex agnus-castus), is the highest-evidence record in this review. Standardised fruit extract has reduced premenstrual syndrome symptoms '
   'relative to placebo in several controlled trials, and the EMA regards this use as well established [1].',
   'The limit of that scope matters: the well-established use is for premenstrual syndrome, not for period pain itself. The two are not the same, and the plant’s '
   'record covers one and not the other.',
   'Chaste tree has a hormonal effect, and this is the source of its restrictions: it may interact with oral contraceptives, hormone therapy and medicines acting on '
   'dopamine. It must not be used during pregnancy or breastfeeding. The TİTCK monograph advises that people who have had, or currently have, an oestrogen-sensitive '
   'cancer should not use it without consulting a physician.']),
 ('Traditional records for period pain',
  ['The level of evidence for the plants recorded for period pain itself is markedly lower than for chaste tree. For {civanpercemi}, yarrow (Achillea millefolium), '
   'the EMA recognises loss of appetite, dyspepsia, period pain and minor wounds under traditional use; human studies are few [1]. Anatolian records note that it is '
   'infused for stomach and period pain.',
   'Yarrow’s restrictions are not slight: it can cause allergy in people allergic to the daisy family, it must not be used in pregnancy, and the TİTCK monograph '
   'advises stopping it two weeks before surgery; it interacts with antiplatelet medicines, blood thinners and lithium.',
   '{abuta} (Abuta grandifolia) is used in Amazonian records for period pain, post-partum bleeding and women’s health. Muscle-relaxant and anti-inflammatory effects '
   'have been seen in laboratory and animal studies; there are no human studies [2]. Because it may act on the uterus it must not be used in pregnancy, and it can '
   'lower blood pressure.']),
 ('Menopause is a separate matter',
  ['Menopausal symptoms do not sit comfortably under the same heading as menstrual complaints; they are a different presentation and a different group of records.',
   '{karayilan}, black cohosh (Actaea racemosa), is this area’s well-established use record: the EMA recognises the root extract for menopausal symptoms such as hot '
   'flushes. NCCIH, for its part, notes that trial results are inconsistent [1][3]. In North America the Cherokee and other indigenous peoples used the root for '
   'women’s health, rheumatism and snakebite.',
   'Rare cases of liver injury have been reported; people with liver disease should not use it. The TİTCK monograph advises against use in pregnancy and '
   'breastfeeding, and that people with breast cancer should use it only under medical supervision.']),
 ('Plants recorded as “bringing on a period”, and the deaths reported',
  ['In folk medicine records some plants appear as means of bringing on a period or of ending a pregnancy. These records are real and belong in an ethnobotanical '
   'catalogue; but being recorded is not the same as being usable. The two highest safety risks in this review are in this group.',
   '{yarpuz}, pennyroyal (Mentha pulegium), carries a high proportion of pulegone in its volatile oil. Pulegone is toxic to the liver and kidneys, and deaths have '
   'been reported from ingestion of the oil [2]. It has been used throughout history in Europe and Latin America to bring on a period and to end a pregnancy. The '
   'decisive point here is this: the toxicity arrives before, or together with, any intended effect. It is not a plant that works but carries risk; it is a poisoning '
   'that can end in liver failure. Nor should it be drunk as a tea heavily or over long periods.',
   '{sedef}, rue (Ruta graveolens), is known in folk medicine as a means of bringing on a period, and in Andean markets it is the central plant of limpia cleansing '
   'rituals under the name ruda. It can cause miscarriage in pregnancy, and at high doses it is toxic to the liver and kidneys. Touching the fresh plant and then '
   'going into sunlight causes severe skin burns (phytophotodermatitis) — its furanocoumarins make skin hypersensitive to sunlight. There is no clinical evidence for '
   'the plant itself [2].',
   'For someone seeking to end a pregnancy, these plants are not a route. In Türkiye the termination of pregnancy is governed by Article 5 of Law no. 2827 on '
   'Population Planning; it is a procedure that may be carried out within ten weeks of the beginning of the pregnancy, with the person’s consent and without their '
   'having to give a reason [4]. The place to go is not a plant but an obstetrician and gynaecologist.']),
 ('The difference between monograph types',
  ['The phrases “well-established use” and “traditional use” above are not synonyms. A herbal product reaches the market in the European Union by different regulatory '
   'routes, two of which diverge at the level of the monograph [1].',
   'On the well-established use route (Directive 2001/83/EC, Article 10a), scientific literature must establish that the active substances have been in '
   'well-established medicinal use within the European Union for at least ten years, with recognised efficacy and an acceptable level of safety [1].',
   'On traditional use registration (Article 16a(1)), the plant must have been in use for at least thirty years, of which at least fifteen within the European Union. '
   'No clinical trials are required; sufficient safety data and plausible efficacy are enough [1].',
   'Two records in this review sit in the upper category, and neither is for period pain: chaste tree for premenstrual syndrome, black cohosh for menopausal symptoms. '
   'The plants recorded for period pain itself remain at the level of traditional use.']),
 ('When to see a doctor',
  ['Period pain is common, but not all pain is the same and some of it has a cause that needs investigating. Herbal use should not delay that investigation.',
   'The following should not be deferred by a herbal product: pain severe enough to prevent daily activity and unresponsive to painkillers, pain that worsens markedly '
   'over time, pain during intercourse or persisting outside the menstrual period, bleeding that requires changing a pad or tampon every one to two hours, bleeding '
   'lasting more than seven days, bleeding between periods, any bleeding after menopause, absence of periods for more than three months (pregnancy aside), and pelvic '
   'pain with fever.',
   'For people taking oral contraceptives, hormone therapy, blood thinners or antidepressants, some of the plants in this review carry interactions and restrictions. '
   'This holds particularly for chaste tree, which has a hormonal effect. The interaction information is given on each plant’s own record page.'])]},
'sindirim-sikayetleri-aci-ve-kokulu-bitkiler': {
'slug': 'digestive-complaints-bitter-and-aromatic-plants',
'baslik': 'Plants used for digestive complaints: two groups, two mechanisms',
'aciklama': 'The plants reached for against indigestion and gas across unrelated cultures fall into two groups: the markedly bitter ones and the aromatic ones rich in '
            'volatile oils. Bitters, carminatives, the clinical evidence for peppermint, the systemic effects of liquorice root, and the difference between EMA monograph '
            'types — with sources.',
'giris': 'The plants used for indigestion and gas across different cultures fall into two sensory groups: the markedly bitter ones and the aromatic ones rich in volatile '
         'oils. The distinction corresponds to the amara and carminative classes of traditional pharmacy and is explained by two different mechanisms of action. This '
         'review covers both groups, their standing in European Medicines Agency monographs and the current level of clinical evidence; it contains no dosage information.',
'bolumler': [
 ('Scope and method',
  ['The plants in this review were selected on two criteria: either the relevant indication is covered by a European Medicines Agency (EMA) herbal monograph, or the '
   'catalogue holds a recorded traditional use for this complaint [1]. The parts used, the safety information and the restrictions for each plant are given on its own '
   'record page. Dosage and preparation have deliberately been left out of this review; that information should not be read apart from the safety restrictions.']),
 ('Two groups, two mechanisms',
  ['Indigestion is among the oldest complaints in recorded medical history, and every independently developed medical tradition has an answer to it. The plants those '
   'traditions reach for are not randomly distributed; they cluster around two sensory properties.',
   'The first group consists of plants with a marked bitterness. The view that bitter compounds stimulate digestive secretions formed the basis of the amara class in '
   'European pharmacy. The second group comprises aromatic plants rich in volatile oils; the relaxing effect of these oils on intestinal smooth muscle is the ground of '
   'the carminative classification. The first group answers to sluggish digestion, the second to tension and bloating.']),
 ('Bitter plants',
  ['The clearest European example of this group is {pelin}, wormwood (Artemisia absinthium); the species name points directly to the bitterness. The EMA monograph for '
   'Absinthii herba covers loss of appetite and mild digestive complaints [1]. Gentler members of the same class are {karahindiba}, dandelion (Taraxacum officinale), '
   'and {civanpercemi}, yarrow (Achillea millefolium); both have monographs, and both were accepted on the basis of traditional use [1].',
   '{devedikeni}, milk thistle (Silybum marianum), holds a separate position in this group. It is better known for the liver studies conducted on the silymarin in its '
   'seeds, yet its monograph covers digestive complaints as well [1].',
   'The evidence for the bitter group can be summarised as follows: the mechanism is defensible, the clinical evidence limited. Experimental data exist on the effect of '
   'bitter compounds on secretion; for most of these plants, however, there are no controlled trials on the scale required for a claim of efficacy. That the monographs '
   'were issued on a traditional-use basis reflects this.']),
 ('Aromatic plants',
  ['The second group has a far wider geographical distribution. In Anatolia {nane}, peppermint (Mentha × piperita), {rezene}, fennel (Foeniculum vulgare), {papatya}, '
   'chamomile (Matricaria chamomilla), {melisa}, lemon balm (Melissa officinalis), {kimyon}, cumin (Cuminum cyminum), and {biberiye}, rosemary (Salvia rosmarinus), are '
   'used for this purpose. In the Andes, around Cusco, {muna} (Minthostachys mollis) is recorded for the same function, and in the Amazon basin {matico} '
   '(Piper aduncum).',
   'There is no close kinship among these species, and no data indicating transmission between the communities that use them. What they share is chemical: all are rich '
   'in volatile oils, and the relaxing effect of these compounds on smooth muscle is common to them. This constitutes a case of convergence, in which separate '
   'traditions arrived at the same group of plants independently of any knowledge of the mechanism.']),
 ('Peppermint: the strongest clinical evidence in the group',
  ['While most of the aromatic plants remain at the level of traditional use, peppermint oil has been the subject of controlled clinical trials. A meta-analysis '
   'published in 2019 pooled twelve randomised trials covering 835 patients and found enteric-coated peppermint oil significantly superior to placebo for the symptoms '
   'of irritable bowel syndrome [2].',
   'An update published in 2022 renewed the analysis, noting that trials published in the interval had called the result into question. The updated finding again '
   'favours peppermint oil; the effect size is stated more cautiously [3]. The catalogue therefore records peppermint at the clinical level of evidence, the only such '
   'case in the digestive group.',
   'The same mechanism carries a limitation. Peppermint oil also relaxes the lower oesophageal sphincter, and may worsen symptoms in people with gastro-oesophageal '
   'reflux. An effect that benefits one group constitutes a contraindication in another.']),
 ('Ginger and turmeric',
  ['{zencefil}, ginger (Zingiber officinale), does not fall squarely into either group; its pungency comes from gingerol derivatives and its effect is discussed in '
   'terms of gastric emptying rate. The EMA monograph for Zingiberis rhizoma covers digestive complaints [1]. The monograph for {zerdecal}, turmeric (Curcuma longa), '
   'carries the same indication [1].',
   'For both plants the difference between culinary and supplement use is decisive. The amounts consumed as a spice are not comparable to those in capsules containing '
   'standardised extract; cases of liver injury associated with high-dose turmeric capsules have been reported. It cannot therefore be assumed that a plant in common '
   'kitchen use carries the same safety profile in supplement form.']),
 ('Liquorice root and its systemic effects',
  ['{meyan}, liquorice root (Glycyrrhiza glabra), falls outside both sensory groups; it is sweet rather than bitter. The EMA monograph for Liquiritiae radix covers '
   'digestive complaints [1].',
   'The plant differs from the other records in this review in its systemic effects. Regular consumption in high amounts raises blood pressure, lowers serum potassium '
   'and can lead to cardiac arrhythmia. It is not recommended for people with hypertension, heart failure or kidney disease, or during pregnancy. Assessing liquorice '
   'root in the same category as the other digestive plants is therefore not appropriate.']),
 ('The difference between monograph types',
  ['The phrase “the EMA monograph covers”, repeated above, does not by itself amount to a claim of efficacy. A herbal product reaches the market in the European Union '
   'by different regulatory routes, two of which diverge at the level of the monograph [1].',
   'On the well-established use route (Directive 2001/83/EC, Article 10a), scientific literature must establish that the active substances have been in well-established '
   'medicinal use within the European Union for at least ten years, with recognised efficacy and an acceptable level of safety [1].',
   'On traditional use registration (Directive 2001/83/EC, Article 16a(1)), the plant must have been in use for at least thirty years, of which at least fifteen within '
   'the European Union. No clinical trials on safety and efficacy are required; sufficient safety data and plausible efficacy are enough [1].',
   'Most of the plants in this review fall into the second category. That a plant has a monograph therefore shows that its use rests on a regulatory framework; it does '
   'not show that its effect has been clinically demonstrated. Keeping the two categories apart is decisive for transmitting knowledge in this field accurately.']),
 ('When to see a doctor',
  ['The great majority of indigestion complaints resolve on their own. This is one of the factors that explains how widespread traditional uses are: when a complaint '
   'resolves by itself, there is a tendency to associate the recovery with whatever was last applied.',
   'The following findings are not assessed as indigestion and must not be deferred by herbal use: difficulty swallowing, haematemesis (blood in vomit), melaena (black '
   'stools), unintentional weight loss, a complaint lasting more than two weeks without remission, pain radiating to the chest, and a complaint beginning for the first '
   'time after the age of fifty.',
   'For people on regular medication, pregnant women and those breastfeeding, some of the plants in this review carry drug interactions and restrictions on use. The '
   'interaction information is given on each plant’s own record page.'])]},
'tarih-oncesinde-bitkiler-arkeoloji': {'slug': 'plants-in-prehistory-archaeology',
 'baslik': 'Plants in prehistory: how archaeology reveals our bond with plants',
 'aciklama': 'Traces of datura and ephedra in 3,000-year-old locks of hair from Menorca, braziers of burned cannabis in the Pamirs, a thousand-year-old shaman’s bundle in the Andes and the domestication of the poppy in '
             'the Mediterranean: with sources.',
 'giris': 'Plants are mostly invisible in the archaeological record: they are eaten, drunk or burned, and little is left behind. Yet in recent years chemical analysis has learned to read plants used thousands of years '
          'ago from residues at the bottom of vessels, and even from human hair. This article gathers a few striking examples from open-access research.',
 'bolumler': [('Finding invisible plants',
               ['For a long time researchers inferred plant use from indirect evidence: mortars, snuff kits, pipes, seeds and pollen. In the eastern Mediterranean, opium alkaloids found in small Late Bronze Age juglets '
                'shaped like upturned poppy capsules supported the idea that the shape advertised the contents [1].',
                'More direct evidence comes from human remains: plant alkaloids have been detected in the hair of pre-Columbian American mummies, in prehistoric bones from China and in the tooth enamel of Iron Age '
                'people in Vietnam. Some older findings are considered controversial because their methods were not described well enough [1].']),
              ('The domestication of the poppy in the Mediterranean',
               ['According to the authors of the Menorca study, the {hashas} was most likely domesticated in the western Mediterranean in the Early Neolithic: remains of the cultivated form have been found at several '
                'sites in Italy, southern France and Spain, and the plant then spread quickly across Europe. At Prats in Andorra, a Middle Bronze Age ritual pit from around 1600 BCE yielded charred {tatula} capsules '
                '[1].']),
              ('Menorca: locks of hair dyed red',
               ['In Menorca, in the Balearic Islands, the cave of Es Càrritx was found untouched in 1995. The chamber at its entrance served as a collective tomb for about 600 years, from 1450 to 800 BCE, and held more '
                'than 200 people. During the last 300 years of the tomb’s use a strange rite took place: the hair of some of the dead was dyed or anointed red, combed, cut and placed in wooden or antler tubes whose lids were carved with '
                'concentric circles [1].',
                'Around 800 BCE some of these tubes were hidden in a small chamber deep in the cave and sealed with clay. Analysis of hair from a tube carved from olive wood, with a boxwood lid, found atropine, '
                'scopolamine and ephedrine. According to the authors the likely sources were a nightshade such as {tatula} and Ephedra fragilis, the island’s only native ephedra; the length of the hair shows the person '
                'took them for about a year before death [1].',
                'The cave holds no remains of these plants, so they were used in another setting, not in the funeral rites. The authors note that handling such toxic plants required specialised knowledge of the kind '
                'typically held by shamans, and link the concentric circles on the lids to eyes, a symbol of “inner vision”, and to the dilated pupils these alkaloids cause. In Menorca today ephedra leaves are still '
                'used in some remedies, and datura and white henbane (Hyoscyamus albus) were once smoked like cigarettes against asthma [1].']),
              ('The Pamirs: cannabis burned in braziers',
               ['Herodotus wrote in the fifth century BCE that people of the Caspian steppe sat in a small tent and burned cannabis on hot stones. A small tent frame and copper vessels with burned stones found in the '
                'Pazyryk kurgans of the Altai (around 500 BCE) seem to support his account [2].',
                'At the Jirzankal cemetery in western China, at over 3,000 metres on the Pamir Plateau (about 2,500 years ago), ten wooden braziers holding burned stones were found in eight tombs. Chemical analysis '
                'showed that {kenevir} had been burned in them and that the plants were high in psychoactive compounds. The authors call it some of the earliest directly dated and scientifically verified evidence for '
                'ritual cannabis smoking. The cemetery also held glass beads and angular harps typical of western Asia and silk from eastern China: the Pamirs were a crossroads of cultures [2].']),
              ('The Andes: a shaman’s bundle and a three-thousand-year-old cactus',
               ['In a roughly 1,000-year-old leather ritual bundle found in a cave in Bolivia, a pouch sewn from three fox snouts held traces of cocaine, DMT, bufotenine and harmine: {koka}, {yopo} and the {ayahuasca} '
                'vine. None of these plants grew in the area [3].',
                'In Peru, remains of the {sanpedro} cactus were found at Guitarrero Cave in the Andes in layers dated to 8200–6800 BCE, and the cactus is often depicted in Chavín and Moche art [4]. The tradition lives '
                'on in the mesa ceremonies of healers in northern Peru today.']),
              ('A warning',
               ['Most of the plants in this article are highly poisonous; datura, henbane and ephedra can cause serious poisoning even in small doses. They are described here as a record of the history of humanity’s '
                'bond with plants.'])]},
'kuzey-peruda-sifa-mesa-san-pedro-pazar': {'slug': 'healing-in-northern-peru-mesa-san-pedro-market',
 'baslik': 'Healing in northern Peru: the mesa altar, San Pedro and the 510 plants of the market',
 'aciklama': 'From the markets of Trujillo and Chiclayo to the healers’ altars: plant healing in northern Peru today, its thousand-year roots, “seguro” bottles, flower baths and the San Pedro cactus. With sources.',
 'giris': 'Northern Peru lies at the upper end of the old Andean “health axis” that runs from southern Ecuador to northern Bolivia. The ethnobotanist Rainer Bussmann and the anthropologist Douglas Sharon worked for '
          'more than a decade in the region’s markets and alongside its healers. This article summarises the region’s healing tradition from their open-access study.',
 'bolumler': [('The plants of the market',
               ['The study began in 2001 in the big markets of Trujillo and Chiclayo. In northern Peru 510 plants with medicinal properties were recorded, with 2,499 different uses; 83 per cent of the species were '
                'native to Peru. Some 150–200 species are commonly sold in local markets; healers collect the rare ones themselves or order them from specialised collectors [1].',
                'Vendors group plants into four kinds: common and exotic plants, plants for ordinary illnesses, plants used only by healers, and plants for magical purposes. About 974 mixtures with up to 29 ingredients '
                'treat 164 conditions; around 65 per cent of the plants are used in mixtures [1].',
                'The tradition is not frozen. Compared with the plants in the collection Bishop Martínez Compañón sent to Spain in 1789, half of the plants used in colonial times have disappeared from the list, yet the '
                'total has grown. The recent spread of noni products in the markets shows that herbalists watch international health trends closely [1].']),
              ('Most plants go to “magical” illnesses',
               ['The largest number of species, 207, is used for “magical” ailments, more than a quarter of all uses. Bad air (mal aire), bad wind, fright (susto or espanto), the evil eye (mal ojo) and sorcery (daño, '
                'brujería) are considered very common illnesses in Andean society. According to the researchers, the Western idea of “psychosomatic disorders” comes closest to describing them [1].',
                'Treatment often involves a cleansing ceremony, a limpia: this can be a simple spraying with holy water and perfume, or an all-night ceremony at the healer’s altar. In the days after the ceremony the '
                'patient takes a “flowering bath” (baño de florecimiento). The patient also receives a “seguro”: a bottle filled with powerful herbs, perfumes, images of saints and the patient’s hair and nail '
                'clippings, carried for protection and luck [1].']),
              ('Mesa: at the altar',
               ['Healers usually treat patients at the mesa altar set up in their back yard, sometimes at sacred places in the countryside or on the shores of sacred lagoons high in the mountains. On the altar, “power '
                'objects” such as seashells, pre-Columbian ceramics, staffs and stones stand side by side with crosses and images of saints [1].',
                'The ceremony begins with the healer spraying blessed plant extracts from his mouth over the patient’s whole body. In most ceremonies the patient drinks boiled {sanpedro} juice and takes tobacco '
                '({mapacho}) juice and perfume through the nose; sometimes {toe} (Brugmansia) is used too. In the chants called tarjos the healers invoke the mountain spirits (apus) and sacred lagoons as well as '
                'Christ, the Virgin and the saints. The guinea pig (cuy) is a standard tool of diagnosis [1].']),
              ('A three-thousand-year-old cactus',
               ['The use of the San Pedro cactus goes back thousands of years. Its remains have been found at Guitarrero Cave in the Andes (8200–6800 BCE), at Las Aldas on the north-central coast (2000–1500 BCE) and at '
                'Garagay on the central coast. The cactus is often depicted in Cupisnique, Chavín, Moche, Nazca and Lambayeque art [1].',
                'Among the plants whose use goes back to the Moche period (100–800 CE), besides San Pedro, are maichil ({camalonga}) and ishpingo: they are depicted on Moche ceramics, and remains of some were found in '
                'elite burials such as the tomb of the Lord of Sipán [1][2]. Despite five hundred years of suppression the tradition survived; in 2022 Peru’s Ministry of Culture declared the traditional knowledge of '
                'San Pedro part of the nation’s cultural heritage [3].']),
              ('Threats',
               ['According to Bussmann and Sharon, the greatest threat to this ancient tradition is the loss of plant habitats: urban sprawl and sugar, cotton and rice agribusiness have changed the coastal plains; '
                'climate change and deforestation threaten the mountain forests. The high Andean ecosystems and sacred lagoons where many medicinal plants grow are endangered by large-scale mining [1].',
                'Demand is growing but cultivation is not: most plants are still gathered from the wild, and some important species show signs of overharvesting. The researchers also tested the plants in the '
                'laboratory: antibacterial activity was confirmed in most plants used for infections, but 24 per cent of water extracts and 76 per cent of alcohol extracts were toxic. Traditional preparation methods '
                'take this into account when choosing the solvent [1]. San Pedro and Brugmansia are powerful and dangerous plants; they are described here as a cultural record.'])]},
'dunya-agaclari-kayin-yggdrasil-ceiba-bodhi': {'slug': 'world-trees-birch-yggdrasil-ceiba-bodhi',
 'baslik': 'World trees: the birch, Yggdrasil, the ceiba and the Bodhi tree',
 'aciklama': 'The birch reaching to the sky in the Altai, the ash that holds up the world in the Edda, the Maya world axis ceiba, the date palm of Assyrian palaces and the fig under which the Buddha '
             'awoke: the tree at the centre of the world, with sources.',
 'giris': 'Cultures thousands of kilometres apart have placed a tree at the centre of the universe. With its roots in the underworld, its trunk on earth and its branches in the sky, this tree joins '
          'the three worlds. This article sets side by side the world trees we describe separately on the site.',
 'bolumler': [('The birch in the Altai',
               ['Turkic mythology has a “cosmic tree” at the centre of the universe reaching to the Pole Star, named in the sources Bay Kayın or Altınkazık [1]. According to Altai tales a birch '
                'rises on the summit of the world mountain. Kams do not perform rites without a birch; in the Altai horse sacrifice they pass through the layers of the sky by climbing a nine-stepped '
                'birch set up in the middle of the tent [1]. In Central Asian Turkic “kayın” means birch.']),
              ('The ash in the Edda: Yggdrasil',
               ['In the Norse Eddic poems the tree that holds up the world is Yggdrasil, an ever-green ash standing at the well of Urd. Its three roots reach over the land of the dead, the frost '
                'giants and humankind. An eagle lives at its top and the dragon Nidhogg at its foot; the squirrel Ratatosk carries words between them [2]. The likeness to the bird at the top and the '
                'serpent at the foot of the Turkic tree of life is striking.']),
              ('The Maya ceiba',
               ['In Classic Maya cosmology the giant ceiba (Ceiba pentandra) is the axis of the world; its roots reach into the underworld and it is said to hold up the world. In Maya, Aztec and '
                'Mixtec art the conical spines of the young ceiba trunk were modelled in clay on incense burners and funerary urns. The ceiba is today the national tree of Guatemala [3].',
                'The same tree is sacred in the Amazon too: healers around Iquitos in Peru count the ceiba, which they call “lupuna”, among the plants that teach medicine when taken with a diet '
                '[4].']),
              ('The date palm in Assyria',
               ['In the palace of the Assyrian king Ashurnasirpal II at Nimrud (9th century BC), stylised trees stand beneath the winged sun-disc, flanked by guardian figures. Norma Franklin '
                'identifies them as female date palms with their offshoots and reads them as a symbol of the king’s lineage [5].']),
              ('The Buddha’s tree: Bodhi',
               ['Siddhartha gained enlightenment under a pipal tree (Ficus religiosa) at Gaya, so the tree became the Bodhi, or wisdom, tree. In early Buddhist reliefs the Buddha is not shown in '
                'human form; he is represented by the Bodhi tree and the empty throne beneath it [6].']),
              ('What do they share?',
               ['All these trees speak of a centre, an axis and a connection: between underworld and sky, between the dead and the living, between people and gods. Tying cloth to a tree in Anatolia '
                'or making a wish at the foot of an old plane tree is one of the forms in which this very old idea survives. Each community’s story is its own; what is reported here is what the '
                'sources say.'])]},
'anadoluda-bitkiyle-tedavinin-tarihi-hititlerden-osmanliya': dict(
    slug='history-of-plant-medicine-in-anatolia',
    baslik='A short history of plant medicine in Anatolia: from the Hittites to the Ottomans',
    aciklama='From the flower burial at Shanidar to Hittite tablets, Seljuk hospitals and the first Ottoman drug books in Turkish: the long journey of healing-plant knowledge in Anatolia.',
    giris='Anyone who buys linden or sage from a herbalist in Turkey today stands at the end of a chain of knowledge that goes back thousands of years in Anatolia. The history chapter of Turhan Baytop’s Türkiye’de Bitkiler ile Tedavi (Plant Healing in Turkey) follows that chain from Neanderthal graves to Ottoman hospitals. This article is a short summary of that chapter.',
    bolumler=[
        ('Prehistory: the flowers of Shanidar', [
            'According to Baytop, people in Anatolia have used plants for healing since the Palaeolithic. As the earliest evidence he points to the plant remains found in Neanderthal graves in Shanidar Cave, just south of Hakkari [1]. Whether those remains were left in a ritual or carried into the cave by animals is still debated.',
        ]),
        ('Mesopotamia: 250 plant drugs', [
            'Sumerian, Akkadian and Assyrian tablets show that healing was carried out by priest-physicians using both magic and medicine. Most remedies were made from plants; beer, wine and butter served as carriers. Care was taken to gather plants at particular times, at night or before sunrise or after sunset, and to dry them in the shade [1].',
            'About 250 plant drugs are known from this period. Plants we still recognise today, such as {adamotu}, henbane, {hashas}, {safran}, thyme, mint and fennel, appear often in the prescriptions [1].',
        ]),
        ('The Hittites: “And so the patient will be well”', [
            'We know Hittite medicine from the tablets found in the archive of their capital Hattusa (Boğazköy). The Hittites saw illness as a punishment from the gods and used magic and medicine together. Most of their remedies were plant-based; the Hittite word for a plant drug was “waššī” [1].',
            'Alongside Anatolian plants such as {alic}, almond, {defne}, {meyan}, {mersin}, {sarimsak} and olive, the prescriptions also mention imported drugs such as myrrh and asafoetida. The Hittites cultivated some plants and sold others, such as saffron and opium poppy, abroad [1].',
            'Amounts are rarely given; the tablets say only “a little” or “much”. Whether the remedy is to be taken by night or by day, however, is stated. Every prescription ends with the sentence “And so the patient will be well”; the next one begins “If he does not get well” [1].',
        ]),
        ('The Seljuks: a hospital in every city', [
            'In the two centuries from 1071 to 1308, the Anatolian Seljuks founded hospitals called bimaristan, darülafiye or darüşşifa in almost every large city. As Baytop reports, these were fully equipped institutions with their own physicians and pharmacists [1].',
            'According to Seljuk endowment deeds, shops selling medicines in the cities stocked plants brought even from India, and caravanserai hospitals had pharmacies of their own. The first of these hospitals is the Gevher Nesibe Sultan Hospital, founded in Kayseri in 1206. The two snake reliefs on its gate can still be seen; the building is now a museum [1].',
        ]),
        ('The Ottomans: herb gatherer, syrup maker and pharmacist', [
            'Ottoman medicine largely continued Seljuk and Islamic medicine; the main difference is that it was written in Turkish rather than Arabic or Persian. The endowment deed of the hospital that Sultan Bayezid I built in Bursa in 1399 names three people who worked with medicines: the şerbetçi, who made syrups and sherbets; the aşşab, who knew the medicinal plants and gathered or bought them; and the saydelan, the pharmacist who prepared pastes, pills and the main medicines [1].',
            'The oldest known drug book written in Turkish is the Edviye-i Müfrede of İshak bin Murad of Gerede, dated 1389–90. In plain Turkish it lists remedies in alphabetical order, with where each is found, what it is good for and what harm it can do. Baytop notes that most of the plants in this book are still known among the people by the same names and used for the same purposes [1].',
            'In the fifteenth century Sabuncuoğlu Şerefeddin, chief physician of the Amasya hospital, ran an experiment to see whether his antidotes worked against snake venom: he had a viper bite a rooster, then gave it the antidote and recorded the result [1]. In the same century the physician Şirvânî, who came to Anatolia from Shirvan, was writing about how to detect counterfeit drugs, including how he told real {zerdecal} from fake.',
        ]),
        ('A note', [
            'This article is a short summary of the history chapter of Baytop’s book; see the book for details and primary sources. It is not always certain which modern species the plant names in old prescriptions refer to.',
        ]),
    ]),
'ogreten-bitkiler-dieta-ayahuasca-pastora': dict(
    slug='teacher-plants-dieta-ayahuasca-pastora',
    baslik='Teacher plants: the dieta in the Amazon and the leaf of the Pastora in the Mazatec mountains',
    aciklama='The dieta healers undertake with “plants that have a mother” in the Peruvian Amazon, the mother of mapacho tobacco who appears in dreams, the language of ritual in ayahuasca tourism, and Salvia divinorum in Mexico: with sources.',
    giris='In some communities a plant is not only a medicine but a teacher. In the Peruvian Amazon, healers believe that certain plants have a “mother” who comes in dreams and visions to teach. In the Mazatec mountains of Mexico, a sacred leaf bears a name that evokes the Virgin Mary. This article draws on field studies from both worlds.',
    bolumler=[
        ('Plants that have a mother', [
            'In a field study carried out between 2003 and 2008 in the east-central Peruvian Amazon, researchers interviewed 29 healers, 3 apprentices and 4 herbalists; some were Shipibo-Konibo, Asháninka and Matsigenka, others from mestizo communities. They recorded 55 plant species from 26 families used in preparing to become a healer [1].',
            'Locally these plants are called “plantas con madre”, plants with a mother. Each is believed to have a spirit, a mother; when the apprentice takes the plant, the mother teaches the uses of plants, the path of healing and sacred knowledge [1].',
        ]),
        ('The dieta: alone in the forest, without salt, in silence', [
            'The path to this learning runs through a regimen called the “dieta”. It has four core elements: withdrawing into the forest and speaking to no one except the master healer; taking one or more plants every day; giving up salt, sugar, fat, pork, game, sour foods, garlic, hot sauce, alcohol and cold drinks, sometimes fasting completely; and sexual abstinence [1].',
            'The master healer gives the plants in a set order. First come plants for purging and cleansing, then plants that open sensitivity and intuition, then strengthening plants, and last the plants that protect and defend [1]. Among other Amazonian peoples too, healers in training seek to reach the spirit world through seclusion, fasting and abstinence [1].',
        ]),
        ('The mother of tobacco', [
            'The strong tobacco called “rome” in Shipibo-Konibo and known locally as mapacho (Nicotiana rustica) is considered one of the most powerful and oldest plants. According to one healer, mapacho balances negative energies in the body and teaches a person to become a warrior who cleanses himself; its mother appears in dreams as a large, short-haired, dark-skinned man in silver-embroidered clothing [1].',
            'Two healers described the mother of tobacco as twins: these twins teach the icaros, the healing songs, and how to blow smoke over the patient [1].',
        ]),
        ('Ayahuasquero or curandero?', [
            'Today people from all over the world travel to the Amazon, especially around Iquitos in Peru, to take part in ayahuasca ceremonies. An ethnographic study in this area shows that local people draw a distinction: someone who leads ayahuasca ceremonies is an “ayahuasquero”, while a “curandero” is a healer who can treat many ailments with many plants. The locals interviewed stressed that not everyone who leads ceremonies is a curandero [2].',
            'The same study examines how the long talks that shamans give before and during ceremonies for tourists frame the experience. These talks set the ceremonial space apart from everyday life and shape how participants make sense of what they go through [2]. The ceremonies also include icaros sung partly in Quechua and other Indigenous languages [2].',
            'We tell the chemistry of the plant pair that makes ayahuasca possible, and why it interacts dangerously with medicines, in a separate story: the drink works because substances in the vine stop the DMT in the chacruna leaf from being broken down in digestion [4].',
        ]),
        ('The leaf of the Pastora in the Mazatec mountains', [
            'Salvia divinorum is a large, velvety-leaved sage that grows wild only in the Mazatec mountains of southern Mexico. The Mazatec call it “xka pastora” in their language, a name that links the plant to the Virgin Mary [3].',
            'Outside the region the plant is known almost only for its psychoactive effect, but locally its use is much broader. People interviewed apply the leaves externally for joint pain, swelling and inflammation, insect stings, sore throat and headache, and drink a mild tea of a few fresh leaves for headache, fever and general pain. Some also use it to treat animals [3].',
            'The Mazatec grow the plant near their homes but keep it out of sight, because it is a sacred being whose rules must be followed. The researcher noticed that the first plant she saw was bent like an arch: in these mountains an arch shows that something is sacred. Arches are also set up around Day of the Dead altars and at the entrances of houses holding weddings and baptisms [3].',
            'The study calls the way drug research reduces the plant to its active compound “stripping”, and the way the Mazatec wrap it in prayers, rules and relationships “dressing”; it stresses that this difference has ethical and political consequences for Indigenous peoples connected to the plant [3].',
        ]),
        ('A note', [
            'This article reports what field studies describe; it is not an account of personal experience or a recommendation for use. Ayahuasca and Salvia divinorum are plants with powerful effects, are regulated in many countries and can interact seriously with medicines.',
        ]),
    ]),
'kutsal-agaclar-kayin-umay-hayat-agaci': dict(
    slug='sacred-trees-birch-umay-tree-of-life',
    baslik='Sacred trees: the birch, Umay and the tree of life',
    aciklama='The birch reaching to the sky among the early Turks, the two trees that came down to earth with Umay, the birch root in the cradle and the custom of tying rags to trees that reached Anatolia: with sources.',
    giris='Someone in Anatolia who ties a strip of cloth to a tree, makes a wish at its foot or cannot bear to see an old plane tree cut down may be following the trace of a very old belief. For the early Turks a tree was not just wood or shade; it was an axis joining earth and sky, the place where a lineage began and the ladder by which the shaman climbed to heaven.',
    bolumler=[
        ('The tree that joins earth and sky', [
            'Turkic mythology has the idea of a “cosmic tree” standing at the centre of the universe and reaching to the Pole Star. In the sources it appears under names such as Demirkazık, Altınkazık, Bay Kayın or the Tree of Saints [1]. Its roots reach into the underworld, its trunk stands on earth and its branches rise into the sky, linking the three worlds [2].',
            'Other cultures have a similar tree. In Norse mythology the tree that holds up the universe is Yggdrasil. In Mesopotamia the tree of life became a symbol of abundance and fertility, and among the Assyrians a symbol of empire [2]. In Turkic narratives a bird, sometimes a double-headed eagle, often perches at the very top of the tree of life [1].',
        ]),
        ('Why the birch?', [
            'The sacred trees most often named in old Turkic narratives are the white pine, larch, birch, cedar, plane, linden and cypress, but the birch comes first [1][2]. According to Altai tales a birch rises on the summit of the world mountain, and in the sacred hollow beneath it lies the water of life [1].',
            'The kams (shamans) of Turkic peoples such as the Sagay, Altai, Shor and Teleut are known not to perform a rite without a birch beside them. Shaman drums almost always show a birch alongside the sun, the moon and lightning. During the rite the branches of the birch stand for the ladder the shaman uses to climb to the sky [1][3]. When a kam died, the drum was broken and hung from the branch of a birch [1].',
            'Kam prayers address the birch directly, calling it the golden-leaved, eight-shadowed, nine-rooted “bay kayın” (rich birch) [1]. The Sagay and Beltir make offerings to the sky or the mountain beneath birch trees; the Sakha hold the larch sacred together with the birch [1].',
            'A small botanical note: in the Turkic languages of Central Asia “kayın” means birch (Betula). In Anatolia the same word is given to a different tree, the beech (Fagus) of Turkish forests. So the white-trunked “kayın” of the myths is in fact the white-barked birch.',
        ]),
        ('The two trees that came down with Umay', [
            'A Sagay kam explained the two birches painted on his drum this way: when we descended from our ancestor Ülgen, these two birch trees came down from the sky to earth together with Mother Umay [1][3]. The sanctity of the birch is therefore tied to creation and to the beginning of the lineage.',
            'In early Turkic belief Umay is the mother figure who helps at birth, protects mother and baby and grants kut, divine favour. In her depictions the birch is among the most frequent symbols, together with hair, comb, thread, bird, bow and arrow; she is also associated with the mountain, the cave, fire and the hearth [3].',
        ]),
        ('The birch root in the cradle', [
            'In the Altai a small birch root was placed in a newborn’s cradle, and the cradle was hung from birch branches; the root of the birch was believed to nourish the baby. If a baby died, it was wrapped in birch bark and laid in a hollow of the tree. The Khakas also laid babies in the hollow of a sacred birch, believing that their souls would go not to the underworld but to the White Milk Lake in the sky [3].',
            'The birch is present as birth approaches too: the shaman stretches horsehair cords in several directions across the tent where the woman lies, hung with figures of the sun, moon and birds cut from birch bark. Among the Teleut, before a child is laid in the cradle for the first time, the oldest woman purifies it with {ardic} smoke [3]. Belief in the cleansing power of juniper smoke is still alive in Anatolia today.',
        ]),
        ('Traces that survive in Anatolia', [
            'While the white pine, larch and birch were held sacred in Turkestan, in Anatolia this reverence passed to the pine, poplar, juniper, cedar, cypress, oak, mulberry, willow, apple and above all the plane tree [1]. Tying coloured rags to trees is one of the forms in which this old tree belief survives today [1].',
            'After Islam the date palm, apple, pomegranate and rose came to the fore. The decorated “nahıl” carried in Ottoman wedding and circumcision processions means date palm [1]. In folk tales one of the most common motifs is a childless couple sharing an apple; the apple stands for wishes fulfilled, children and beauty. The mulberry planted in gardens and cemeteries is part of everyday life through its shade, its fruit and the wood it gives for the saz [4].',
        ]),
        ('Wishing for a child, and the tree', [
            'The link between trees and fertility also appears in the birth rituals of the Shamanist Turks. It is reported that among the Kazakhs childless women rolled under an apple tree in order to conceive, and that among the Kyrgyz people visited sacred trees when wishing for a child [5]. The wheat and sugar thrown over a bride’s head at Anatolian weddings today is seen as a continuation of the custom, among farming Turkic communities, of throwing wheat, millet and fruit over the bride [5].',
        ]),
        ('A note', [
            'The beliefs in this article come from narratives of Turkic peoples recorded at different times and from academic studies of those narratives. Not every community believes the same things; what is reported here is what the sources say.',
        ]),
    ]),
'samanizm-ve-kam-gelenegi': dict(
    slug='what-is-shamanism-kam-tradition',
    baslik='What is shamanism? The kam tradition from Siberia to Anatolia',
    aciklama='The origin of the word shaman, the kam tradition of the Turks, the drum and the sacred tree, juniper smoke and the traces that survive in Anatolia: with sources.',
    giris='The word “shaman” is used very broadly today, for everyone from healers in the Amazon to weekend workshops. Yet the word has an origin and the concept a particular geography. In this article we look at what shamanism is, at the “kam” tradition of the Turks and at the ties this tradition has with plants.',
    bolumler=[
        ('Origin and meaning of the word', [
            'Shamanism is a religious phenomenon centred on a person, the shaman, who is believed to gain power through trance or ecstasy. The shaman is believed to heal illness, to communicate with the spirit world and often to guide the souls of the dead to that world [1].',
            'The word comes from the Tungusic languages of Siberia. A link with the Sanskrit “śramaṇa” (ascetic) has also been proposed, though some researchers reject it [2].',
        ]),
        ('The kam among the Turks', [
            'In Altai and Tatar the shaman is called “kam”. The word appears in Old Uyghur texts, in the Kutadgu Bilig and in the Codex Cumanicus [2]. One of the most comprehensive Turkish-language studies of shamanism in the Turkic world is Abdülkadir İnan’s Tarihte ve Bugün Şamanizm (Shamanism in History and Today), published in 1954 [3].',
            'In the shamanist world view the universe is usually divided into three layers: sky, earth and underworld. Among the Altai the benevolent sky being is Ülgen, and the evil ruler of the underworld is Erlik Khan [2].',
        ]),
        ('Drum and tree: the road to the sky', [
            'The kam’s most important instrument is the drum. Among the Altai the drum, called “tüngür” or “çaluu”, is made after the call to become a kam has been received; its frame is of birch or cedar chosen from untouched forest. The sun, the moon, animals and trees are painted on its face; Altai drums also show the horse that carries the kam to the sky [2].',
            'In this world the tree is not merely a material but a road. In the Altai horse sacrifice recorded by the linguist Wilhelm Radloff in 1884 and retold by Mircea Eliade, a young birch with nine steps cut into it is set up in the middle of a newly pitched tent. Climbing the steps one by one, the kam passes through the layers of the sky and reaches the realm of Bay Ülgen [4].',
            'Among the early Turks some trees such as the birch were held sacred; shamanists believed that their ancestors came from trees and that their souls lived in trees [5].',
        ]),
        ('Smoke and plants', [
            'In shamanic ceremonies plants are often bound up with smoke. In Kyrgyz, Kazakh and Mongolian shamanism {ardic} (archa) smoke drives away evil spirits; before ceremonies the place and the people are purified with archa. Mongolian and Siberian peoples also use {pelin} (Artemisia) smoke for purification.',
            'In north-eastern Siberia, Koryak, Chukchi and Evenk shamans are recorded as using dried {sinekmantari} to enter trance and journey to the spirit world. This mushroom is poisonous; it appears here only as a cultural record.',
        ]),
        ('Traces that survive in Anatolia', [
            'Many Turkic communities lived with shamanism before adopting Buddhism, Manichaeism or Islam. The language historian Zeynep Korkmaz examines how some elements of the old Turkic tree cult continued into the Islamic period [5].',
            'According to Ülkü Gürsoy, tying cloth to the branches of a mulberry tree while making a wish is a custom rooted in old Turkic belief; in the Alevi-Bektashi tradition the mulberry has kept its sanctity and is considered “the soul of the house” [6]. The French Turkologist Irène Mélikoff also drew attention to the shamanic roots of the semah of the Forty in Anatolian Alevism [2].',
            'The {uzerlik} hung at the door, the seeds burned on the hearth and the cloth tied to a tree mostly live on today as “custom”; their roots reach thousands of kilometres east, to the steppe and the taiga.',
        ]),
        ('A note', [
            'Shamanism is not a single religion but a common name for many similar local traditions. On Bitki Tabanı these traditions are described as a cultural record, with the names of the communities that keep them alive and with sources; they are not recommendations for ritual use.',
        ]),
    ]),
'gobekli-tepede-ne-yediler-tarimdan-once': {'slug': 'gobekli-tepe-plants-before-farming',
 'baslik': 'What did they eat at Göbekli Tepe? Anatolia’s plants before farming',
 'aciklama': 'The flotation tanks at Göbekli Tepe produced wild einkorn, wild barley, wild almond and terebinth — and not a single domesticated grain. What the excavation actually shows.',
 'giris': 'Twelve thousand years ago, on a hill northeast of Şanlıurfa, people raised stone pillars weighing tonnes. Those who did it were not yet farmers. The plant remains from the excavation show this odd situation directly: every cereal found at Göbekli Tepe is wild. And yet they left more than seven thousand grinding tools at the same place. They processed grain on a large scale without sowing it.',
 'bolumler': [
    ('What came out of the flotation tank', [
        'The only published archaeobotanical study of Göbekli Tepe is Reinder Neef’s preliminary report covering the 1997–2003 seasons. One thousand nine hundred and fifty litres of soil were washed, and this is what was left: wild einkorn grains and spikelet forks, wild barley, lentil, pistachio, almond, legumes, and a few seeds of the beard-grass and hyacinth types [1].',
        'The names on that list need care, because none of them is the form sold in a shop today. The pistachio in the remains is not {antepfistigi} but its wild relative, terebinth. The lentil is half of a single seed: 2.1 millimetres across, that is, within the range of wild lentil [1]. The barley grains are flat and broadly furrowed — wild barley.',
        'The charcoal list tells the same story: mostly terebinth and almond wood, a little oak, some hawthorn or pear type, and water-loving trees such as ash, poplar and willow [1]. So the hill stood in steppe with scattered trees, and along the nearby streams there were moisture-loving trees.',
        'The most important sentence in the report is this: there is no evidence of domestication in the plant material found [1]. None of the cereals is domesticated. The break scars on the spikelet forks are smooth — the mark of a wild plant whose ear shatters by itself.',
        'One more detail: during the excavation Neef also recorded the hill’s present-day vegetation. The slopes of the Göbekli Tepe plateau are still covered by a grass steppe dominated by wild barley [1]. The plant those people gathered eleven and a half thousand years ago still stands on the same slope.',
    ]),
    ('Processing without sowing', [
        'Few plant remains, many tools. More than seven thousand grinding and pounding tools have been recorded at Göbekli Tepe [2]. For a long time nobody could explain why so many tools sat alongside so few seeds.',
        'The answer came from phytoliths. A phytolith is a microscopic silica cast formed in plant tissue that survives after the plant decays. In the Göbekli Tepe samples, phytoliths from the upper parts of the cereal — the ear and the upper stem — were markedly more common than those from the lower stems [3]. That suggests the ears were cut a few centimetres down the stalk and carried away.',
        'So these people were not sowing grain, but they were gathering, carrying, cleaning and grinding it. A pre-agricultural society’s relationship with cereals does not begin with the verb “to sow”.',
    ]),
    ('Where was einkorn domesticated?', [
        'Northeast of Göbekli Tepe, in the same landscape, stands a volcanic mountain: Karacadağ. A genetic study published in Science in 1997 showed that the wild lines closest to domesticated einkorn wheat were samples collected from this region, and proposed that domestication may have happened here [4].',
        'That finding was not overturned in later years, but it was refined. A broad survey in 2007 did not find the genetic bottleneck expected from domestication, and raised the possibility of several domestications from scattered populations rather than a single event in a single place [5]. A 2018 study looking at the genes that prevent the ear from shattering moved the closest wild lines a little further west, between Kahramanmaraş and Gaziantep [6].',
        'An einkorn genome study published in Nature in 2023 pointed back to the area around Karacadağ and supported the view that domestication happened from a small, limited wild population [7]. The same study showed something else: the story of domesticated einkorn does not end at Karacadağ. On average about seven per cent of its genome came later from wild relatives in central and northwestern Anatolia [7].',
        'In short: Göbekli Tepe was still standing while {bugday} was being domesticated, but no domesticated wheat has been found on the hill. They are two separate moments in the same landscape.',
    ]),
    ('The beer argument, and how it ended', [
        'Large limestone vats holding up to a hundred and sixty litres were found at Göbekli Tepe. A paper published in 2012 argued, on the strength of oxalate residue on their walls, that a fermented cereal drink — beer — may have been prepared here; the paper’s own phrasing was “almost certain” [8]. The claim spread fast: “the world’s first brewery”.',
        'Eight years later the same excavation team tested its own claim. A 2020 study combining chemical analysis, experimental archaeology and tool-use traces found traces of heated cereal in the vats but no firm evidence of fermentation. Oxalate, it noted, can also form through regular contact with water or from other plants, and so is not diagnostic [9].',
        'The team’s 2021 monograph is blunter: the analyses clearly show that large quantities of crushed cereal were processed and prepared by heating — not fermented [2]. The primary interpretation today is not beer but porridge.',
        'This is less the refutation of a claim than an example of how science works. The people who made the claim are the same people who tested it and withdrew it.',
    ]),
    ('Are there plants on the stones?', [
        'The iconography of Göbekli Tepe is overwhelmingly animal: snake, fox, wild boar, crane, scorpion, vulture. Depictions of plants are almost absent.',
        'The one carving that might be read as a plant is the geometric frieze on the head of Pillar 43. It has been suggested that the motifs, which look at first like opposed triangles, are in fact two rows of tassel shapes, that one row resembles a sheaf, and that they evoke grass anatomy — a short ear on a long thin stalk [2].',
        'But this reading is not settled. The researcher who proposed it calls it explicitly “hypothetical” and adds this: the real interest of the Göbekli Tepe artists was not landscape or plants but animals [2].',
    ]),
    ('What is not true', [
        'Most of the plant-related claims circulating about Göbekli Tepe have no source. Four are worth separating out.',
        '“Farming was practised here.” No. There is no evidence of domestication in the plant remains from the excavation; every cereal is wild [1].',
        '“The world’s first brewery.” Not supported. The excavation team’s own analyses found no firm evidence of fermentation [9].',
        '“Psychoactive plants were used here.” There is neither such a claim nor any evidence in the peer-reviewed literature. No psychoactive plant is among the taxa identified at the site [1][2].',
        '“Traces of a lost civilisation, the Garden of Eden, visitors from elsewhere.” This is pseudoscience; the archaeological literature names these claims as such [10]. The claim that figures on the pillars record a comet impact has also been answered in detail by the excavation team: the pillars were moved and reused, the date proposed is centuries earlier than the structure’s earliest radiocarbon date, and the reading lifts a single stone out of its context [11].',
        'A note on Karahantepe as well: no archaeobotanical data have been published in a peer-reviewed form to date. The dietary details that appear in the press rest on studies not yet published. This page will be updated when they are.',
    ]),
 ]},
'bolivyada-bin-yillik-saman-kesesi': {'slug': 'thousand-year-old-shamans-pouch-bolivia',
 'baslik': 'A shaman’s pouch: a thousand-year-old trace of ayahuasca in Bolivia',
 'aciklama': 'A leather bag found in a rock shelter at 3,890 metres held harmine, DMT, bufotenine and cocaine. What the chemistry says — and what it does not.',
 'giris': 'Looking for plant remains in archaeology usually means washing soil, counting seeds and measuring them. But sometimes the plant itself disappears and only its chemistry is left. A thousand-year-old leather bag found in a rock shelter in southwestern Bolivia is the most striking example: in a sample scraped from the surface of a small pouch inside it, two of the essential components of the drink we now call ayahuasca were detected together. This article is about that find — and just as much about where the four researchers who published it stopped and said “we do not know this”.',
 'bolumler': [
    ('A rock shelter at 3,890 metres', [
        'The find comes from the Lípez highlands of southwestern Bolivia, in the valley of the Sora River. The valley is a narrow basin squeezed between two parallel ignimbrite surfaces running north to south. One of the rock shelters cut into those surfaces is known locally as Cueva del Chileno; it lies 3,890 metres above sea level. It was excavated in 2008 and 2010 [1].',
        'The shelter holds traces of intermittent occupation spread over the last four thousand years; the earliest measurement reaches 2136–1778 BC. But the bag this article is about is much later: a small sample taken from the leather was dated by accelerator mass spectrometry and returned a calibrated range of AD 905–1170 [1].',
        'One detail deserves care here. What was dated is the leather of the bag. The plant fragment inside it, the fox-snout pouch and the wooden tablets were not dated separately. So it is more accurate to say “a thousand-year-old bag” than “a thousand-year-old compound”.',
        'In period terms this range falls at the time when the Tiwanaku state broke up and regional polities took its place. The paper does not label the pouch as a Tiwanaku find; the style of the snuff tablets is linked to the Tiwanaku sphere, and that is all [1].',
    ]),
    ('What was inside the bag', [
        'The paper’s inventory runs as follows: an outer leather bag (280 × 165 millimetres); two wooden snuff tablets, skilfully carved and decorated with anthropomorphic figurines; an anthropomorphic snuff tube with two braids of real human hair tied to it; a hide pouch made by sewing together three Andean fox snouts; two llama bone spatulas; two small dried plant fragments tied to cords of wool and plant fibre; and a multicoloured woven headband [1].',
        'That inventory holds ten items. Outside the pouch, in the debris, loose hair braids, turquoise beads and a grinding stone were also found — these are not part of the pouch [1].',
        'Analysis was carried out on only two of those ten items: the fox-snout pouch and the dried plant fragment. The tablets, the tube, the spatulas, the headband and the leather bag itself were not tested. This is the detail most often skipped when the find is described: the compounds came not from the wooden tablets but from a sample scraped off the pouch’s surface.',
    ]),
    ('The compounds found in the pouch', [
        'The method was liquid chromatography–tandem mass spectrometry. Five compounds were detected in the sample scraped from the fox-snout pouch: cocaine, benzoylecgonine (a breakdown product of cocaine), bufotenine, harmine and dimethyltryptamine, that is, DMT [1].',
        'The dried plant fragment yielded only three of those five: cocaine, benzoylecgonine and bufotenine. Harmine and DMT are absent from that sample [1].',
        'A sixth compound, psilocin, is described more cautiously. The paper notes that psilocin was not on the initial screening list, but that a peak corresponding to it appeared and suggests it was present in the sample; it also mentions, as one possibility, that the source could be a psilocin-containing mushroom [1]. The paper’s own abstract does not count psilocin. So “six compounds were found” would be wrong: five compounds were detected, and in addition a peak corresponding to psilocin was observed.',
    ]),
    ('Which compound points to which plant', [
        'For cocaine and benzoylecgonine the paper speaks directly: they show that {koka} leaf was carried in the pouch [1].',
        'Bufotenine is a distinguishing compound. The paper reads it as evidence that seeds of Anadenanthera — the genus of {yopo} and its relatives — were carried in the pouch [1]. Seeds of this genus have also been identified directly under the microscope in archaeological snuff powders: Anadenanthera colubrina var. cebil has been detected in powdered archaeological material in northwestern Argentina [8].',
        'DMT, by contrast, is not distinguishing. The paper leaves two possibilities open: either the DMT comes from the same Anadenanthera material that produced the bufotenine, or from an independent source — a Psychotria species such as {chacruna}, for instance [1]. So “DMT was found” does not mean “chacruna was found”.',
        'For harmine the paper’s reasoning is comparative: among South American plants, harmine occurs in the greatest quantity in the {ayahuasca} vine, Banisteriopsis caapi [1]. Honesty is needed here: harmine and harmaline also occur in other plants such as {uzerlik}, and the main alkaloids of that plant are precisely harmalol, harmaline and harmine [7]. But the paper never discusses Syrian rue or any other source of harmine; it limits its statement to “among South American plants”.',
    ]),
    ('Why two plants are needed', [
        'The most discussed aspect of the find is that harmine and DMT appeared together, because these two are the essential components of the drink called ayahuasca. So why two separate plants?',
        'The mechanism was described in 1984. Banisteriopsis caapi contains harmine, harmaline and tetrahydroharmine; the admixture plants supply the DMT. DMT is a powerful hallucinogen but is inactive by mouth, because it is broken down in the digestive system by the enzyme monoamine oxidase. Beta-carbolines are strong and reversible inhibitors of that enzyme; it has been proposed that they protect DMT from breakdown and so make it orally active [3]. The same study adds two further cautions: as of 1984 the mechanism had not been confirmed experimentally, and the combined effect of the beta-carbolines was found to be additive rather than synergistic [3]. The mechanism was measured in humans in 2003 [4].',
        'The content of the plants is not fixed either. In samples collected from twenty-two separate locations in Brazil, harmine, harmaline and tetrahydroharmine were present in detectable amounts in every Banisteriopsis caapi sample; but in some Psychotria viridis samples DMT was either very low or absent [5].',
        'This mechanism also has a direct medical counterpart, and it needs saying here: a warning published in 1998 holds that beta-carbolines inhibiting monoamine oxidase carry a risk of serious adverse interaction with serotonin reuptake inhibitor medicines [6]. What makes these plants orally active is the same thing that makes them risky.',
    ]),
    ('What the paper does not say', [
        'Some of the sentences circulating about this find go beyond the paper. The authors’ own cautions are these.',
        'The compounds may be external contamination. The paper writes that the tested sample “may reflect the chemistry of objects that plant surface came into contact with”, and that “some, perhaps all” of these compounds may have come from outside. It also states plainly that more analysis is needed to identify the species of the dried plant fragment [1].',
        'There is no firm evidence for ayahuasca. In the paper’s own words: the results do not provide conclusive evidence for early use of ayahuasca in the form seen among indigenous South American communities today — with Banisteriopsis and Psychotria used together — but they are evidence that this combination may have been in use a thousand years ago [1].',
        'It was not shown that these plants were actually consumed. There are no human remains in the context of the find, and the paper states that there is no evidence of direct consumption [1].',
        'The route of use cannot be read from the chemistry. The two snuff tablets, the tube and the spatulas in the kit point strongly to a snuff route, and the authors propose that route for the Anadenanthera seeds; but for the harmine-and-DMT combination they leave open both a drink and a compound snuff [1].',
        'And this is not “the earliest evidence”. The paper does not say “first” or “earliest”. Its claim is quantitative: the largest number of compounds obtained to date from a single find in this part of the world, and the highest number of psychoactives reported in a single archaeological kit in South America [1]. The phrase “conclusive evidence” is used only for the presence of at least three psychoactive plants; for their being used together, the paper says “may have been” [1].',
    ]),
    ('Not a single find', [
        'If Cueva del Chileno stood alone it would be hard to interpret. It does not.',
        'Northeast of Lake Titicaca, in the Eastern Cordillera at about 3,500 metres, a collective tomb at a place called Niño Korin produced five wooden tablets, reed tubes, enema equipment and spatulas; the tablets are in Tiwanaku style. The 1972 monograph that published the find describes it as the kit of a “medicine man” [12]. We could not find a consistent published value for its dating, so we give no date here.',
        'The “Pallqa” pouch, found in 1998 in the Amaguaya valley near La Paz and attributed to the Tiwanaku period, belongs to the same class of find: it has been studied as the medicine bundle of a priest or healer [13].',
        'The chemical side does not rest on a single study either. In northern Chile, harmine has been detected in human hair from the Middle Period (AD 500–1000) [10]. That study carries a notable caution of its own: harmine found alone may point to medicinal rather than hallucinogenic use. The pre-Hispanic snuff powders of San Pedro de Atacama were analysed chemically in 1991 [9]. Which plant supplied the snuff used at Chavín de Huántar has been argued through iconography [11] — the archaeological and historical trace of the {sanpedro} cactus in that region is a literature of its own.',
        'For {koka} the chain runs further back still. Coca leaves found on house floors in the Nanchoc valley of northern Peru have been dated to at least 8,000 calibrated years ago; alongside them were pieces of calcite used to extract the alkaloid from the leaf [14]. A distinction matters here: this date comes from plant remains and context, not from hair or dental analysis.',
    ]),
    ('What a palm-sized pouch says', [
        'The most thought-provoking thing about the find is not the number of compounds but where they came from. None of the plants the pouch points to grows on that highland at 3,890 metres. In the paper’s own words: the presence together of several plants from disconnected and distant ecological zones of South America suggests that hallucinogenic plants were carried over significant distances, and that a detailed botanical knowledge sat within pre-Columbian ritual practice [1].',
        'So this bag is not a plant list but the evidence of a network. Someone had to know which plant grew where, which worked together with which, and how to have it brought from hundreds of kilometres away.',
        'The commentary published in the same issue describes the find as “an ancient pharmacopeia, complete with tools for administering the medicines”, and draws attention to two points: that the key alkaloids of ayahuasca occur in a 1,000-year-old bundle fills the gap left by the hair analyses of Andean mummies, in which no DMT was found; and that the snuffing tablets and tube in the bundle suggest these plants may have been snuffed before they were brewed as a drink. The author treats the headband as a possible shamanic insignia, but leaves this as an open question rather than a conclusion [2].',
        'This article describes an archaeological and historical finding; it is not a recommendation for use. Safety, legal status and drug interaction information for the plants is on their own pages.',
    ]),
 ]},
}
